    The component of $Y_i$ orthogonal to $\rm col(\Lambda_D)$ is independent Gaussian noise and carries no information about $g$. Thus, estimation of $g$ in this oracle submodel is statistically equivalent to estimation from 
    $$X_1,\dots, X_n \overset{iid}{\sim} q_{g,D} := g \star \phi_{D^{-1} I_d}.$$
     We establish the two terms in the lower bound separately.

    \medskip
    \noindent
    \textbf{Step 2: Usual nonparametric lower bound}.
    Considering the more informative experiment in which estimator 
    $\tilde{g}$ is based on 
    directly observed
    $\eta_1,\dots,\eta_n$. The experiment \eqref{eq: modified experiment} is obtained from these observations by applying independently the Markov kernel $\eta \mapsto \eta + D^{-1/2} Z$. Consequently, by monotonicity of minimax risk under such a transform:
    $$\mathfrak{R}_{n,D} \geq \inf_{\tilde{g}}\sup_{g\in \mathcal{G}_{\beta}(L,C)} \mathbb{E}_g \left[ \inf_{R\in\mathcal{O}(d)} \|\tilde{g} - g^R\|_{L^2}^2\right].$$
    To show that the right side is the usual classical nonparametric rate $n^{-2\beta/(2\beta+d)}$, we argue that a slight modification of the classical Assouad construction (accounting for the quotient metric) works. See Lemma \ref{lemma: low dim minimax rotation}. 




    \medskip
    \noindent
    \textbf{Step 3: Construction of two high-frequency alternatives}. Take as baseline $g_0(x)=\phi_d(x)$. For $T\to \infty$, let $F_T$ be the moment-corrected oscillatory function from Lemma \ref{lemma: moment correction} and define

