    \textbf{Controlling KL} Consider two neighboring alternatives $\theta,\theta'$ such that $H(\theta,\theta')=1$. Then $g_\theta$ and $g_{\theta'}$ differ only on one bump. Since $g_*$ is bounded below on $K$ and $a$ is sufficiently small, $g_\theta(x)\asymp g_*(x)$ uniformly on the support of the perturbation. Using ${\sf KL}(f\Vert g) \leq \chi^2(f,g)= \int (f-g)^2/g$, we obtain
    $${\sf KL}(g_\theta \Vert g_{\theta'}) \lesssim \epsilon_h^2 \int \psi_{j,h}(x)^2 dx \lesssim \epsilon_h^2 h^d.$$
    Since Gaussian convolution is a Markov kernel, ${\sf KL}(q_{\theta,D} \Vert q_{\theta',D})\leq {\sf KL}(g_\theta \Vert g_{\theta'})$. Thus, for $n$ independent observations,
    $${\sf KL}\left(q_{\theta,D}^{\otimes n}\;\Vert\; q_{\theta',D}^{\otimes n} \right) \lesssim n\epsilon_h^2 h^d  \leq a^2$$
    by using the definition of $\epsilon_h$. Hence, choosing $a$ sufficiently small makes the total variation distance between every pair of neighboring $n-$sample experiments uniformly bounded away from 1. Assouad's lemma therefore yields
